\title{pinnr: Physics-Informed Neural Networks in Native R}
\description{
\code{pinnr} trains neural networks whose outputs satisfy a differential
equation. A network \eqn{u_\theta(x)} is fitted by minimising a weighted
sum of
\itemize{
\item the mean squared equation residual at collocation points inside the
domain,
\item the mean squared violation of boundary and initial conditions, and
\item the mean squared misfit to observed data, when data are supplied.
}
}
\details{
Unknown constants in the equation can be estimated jointly with the
network weights, which turns the same machinery into a tool for
parameter discovery (an inverse problem).

The main entry points are:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=pinn_ode]{pinn_ode()}} builds a problem from a \code{deSolve}-style derivative function.
\item \code{\link[=pinn_problem]{pinn_problem()}} builds a problem for any partial differential equation
on a rectangular domain defined with \code{\link[=pinn_domain]{pinn_domain()}}, with conditions
from \code{\link[=pinn_dirichlet]{pinn_dirichlet()}}, \code{\link[=pinn_neumann]{pinn_neumann()}}, \code{\link[=pinn_initial]{pinn_initial()}} and
\code{\link[=pinn_condition]{pinn_condition()}}.
\item \code{\link[=pinn_fit]{pinn_fit()}} trains the network.
\item \link[=predict.pinn_fit]{predict()}, \link[=coef.pinn_fit]{coef()},
\link[=residuals.pinn_fit]{residuals()} and \link[=plot.pinn_fit]{plot()}
inspect the result.
}
}
